@h1 A bibliography across files h1@

Let each chapter declare its own sources and have one bibliography assemble
itself at the end. Add a chapter and the list grows on its own — no central
index to maintain.

@h2 Step 1 — Register an entry per chapter h2@

An @strong additive partial strong@ — a definition whose name begins with a
leading plus — merges instead of overwriting when the same name is declared more
than once.

@@@pre
~ in chapters/01-introduction.wit

+#bibliography: @entry Berger, John (1972). Ways of Seeing. Penguin. entry@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 2 — Wrap each entry h2@

Give each entry a tiny value definition so one partial renders as one clean
block. Without the wrapper, inline runs fragment into stray paragraphs.

@@@pre
~ in shared/schema.wit

#entry: ... !!
pre@@@

@h2 Step 3 — Emit the merged list once h2@

Place the assembled list wherever you want it — usually a References section in
the final chapter.

@@@pre
~ in chapters/04-conclusion.wit

@section |title References|
@bibliography bibliography@
pre@@@

@h2 Step 4 — Build the master h2@

@@@pre
wit build master.wit --fragment
pre@@@

@strong Result. strong@ Every additive declaration across the chapters merges,
and the single emit in the conclusion renders the combined list. The worked
@code examples/thesis code@ does exactly this and builds green.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong Keep entry titles plain. strong@ Inline emphasis inside an
additive-partial body fragments the entry into one paragraph per run — the
working example uses plain-text titles for exactly this reason. li@
@li @strong An additive list isn't a collection. strong@ You can't iterate a
merged bibliography with a loop — it is a node definition, not data. To iterate a
list of sources, keep a separate collection literal alongside it. li@
ul@

@h2 Variations h2@

The @strong shared-anchor strong@ pattern: define full citations once in
@code shared/sources.wit code@, reference it from every chapter, cite short by
idea in prose, and list the full entries at the end. This gives you in-text
citations and a bibliography from one source of truth.

@aside(class note) 💡 The self-registering additive-partial example shipped in
the repo is the aspirational shape but currently fails to build. Ground your work
on @code examples/thesis code@, which merges and renders green. aside@

@h2 See also h2@

@a |href /docs/recipes/manuscript-chapters/| A manuscript in chapters a@ is the
multi-file spine this builds on;
@a |href /docs/recipes/thesis/| A thesis to a print-ready PDF a@ covers a
single-file Works Cited list.
